  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "bCZL49eOaQ/Jg8Gv9W5esEJAqPw=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:34:45.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The `DocumentToolbar` component provides a layered styling architecture with clear separation of concerns:\n\n## Layer 1: Containers\nThe toolbar's outer container (background, border, rounding) is controlled via the `toolbarClassName` prop, and each controls container via `controlsContainerClassName`. Both use `twMerge` to merge custom classes with defaults. Default outer classes: `flex h-12 w-full items-center justify-between rounded-xl border border-border bg-background px-4`. Either container component can also be replaced outright with `toolbarContainer` / `controlsContainer`.\n\n## Layer 2: Buttons\nInternal buttons (undo, redo, download, history, close) accept `ToolbarButtonProps` \u2014 `document`, `children`, `className`, `onClick` \u2014 so they can be restyled and rebehaved directly. Each one is also exported (`ToolbarUndoButton`, `ToolbarDownloadButton`, ...), so the usual route is to style the button yourself and pass it back through `componentOverrides` (or render it inside `children`). Arbitrary child selectors are no longer necessary.\n\n## Layer 3: Revision History Panel\nThe revision history panel is rendered by Connect outside the editor component tree. It lives inside `#document-editor-context` in the DOM and can only be styled via scoped CSS in `style.css`.\n\nThis three-layer architecture means theming a toolbar requires understanding which technique applies at each level \u2014 `toolbarClassName` / `controlsContainerClassName` for the containers, per-button `className` or `componentOverrides` for buttons, and external CSS for the history panel.\n\n_Corrected 2026-08-13 against Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:34:45.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:34:45.000Z"
    }
  },
